# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aristotle-politics-3.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1 and aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1.3 have "man_eq_magistrate" and "citizen_obeys" and "magistrate_diff_citizen" and "man_eq_magistrate ∧ citizen_obeys ∧ magistrate_diff_citizen → ¬man_eq_citizen"; aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1.2 has "magistrate_diff_citizen"; they differ when only "the virtues of a good man and a good magistrate are the same", "a citizen is one who obeys the magistrate", "the virtue of the magistrate is different from the virtue of the citizen" and "the virtue of the good man is in general the same as the virtue of the citizen" are true, which the second allows
aristotle-politics-3.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1 and aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "the virtues of a good man and a good magistrate are the same", "a citizen is one who obeys the magistrate", "the virtue of the magistrate is different from the virtue of the citizen" and "the virtue of the good man is in general the same as the virtue of the citizen" are true. Is S2, "But if we allow, that the virtues of a good man and a good magistrate may be the same, and a citizen is one who obeys the magistrate, it follows that the virtue of the one cannot in general be the same as the virtue of the other, although it may be true of some particular citizen; for the virtue of the magistrate must be different from the virtue of the citizen.", true then?
aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1 has "man_eq_magistrate → man_only_command"; aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1.2 and aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1.3 have "man_eq_magistrate ∧ magistrate_diff_citizen → ¬man_eq_citizen"; they differ when only "the virtues of a good man and a good magistrate are the same", "the virtue of the magistrate is different from the virtue of the citizen", "the virtue of the good man is in general the same as the virtue of the citizen" and "the virtue of a good man consists only in being able to command" are true, which the first allows
aristotle-politics-3.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 3 ways: aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1 has "¬same_commendation"; aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1.2 has "man_only_command → ¬same_commendation"; they differ when only "the commendation of the good man and of the good citizen is the same" is true, which the second allows; aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1.3 has "¬man_eq_citizen"
aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1 and aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1.2 put it at S5, "If then the virtue of a good man consists only in being able to command, but the virtue of a good citizen renders him equally fit for the one as well as the other, the commendation of both of them is not the same."; aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1.3 at S2, "But if we allow, that the virtues of a good man and a good magistrate may be the same, and a citizen is one who obeys the magistrate, it follows that the virtue of the one cannot in general be the same as the virtue of the other, although it may be true of some particular citizen; for the virtue of the magistrate must be different from the virtue of the citizen."
aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1: argue/answered: M: answered yes, which keeps aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1 and aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1.2, to: Is the text's main conclusion S5, "If then the virtue of a good man consists only in being able to command, but the virtue of a good citizen renders him equally fit for the one as well as the other, the commendation of both of them is not the same.", rather than S2, "But if we allow, that the virtues of a good man and a good magistrate may be the same, and a citizen is one who obeys the magistrate, it follows that the virtue of the one cannot in general be the same as the virtue of the other, although it may be true of some particular citizen; for the virtue of the magistrate must be different from the virtue of the citizen."?
aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1 takes for granted "if the virtues of a good man and a good magistrate are the same, then the virtue of a good man consists only in being able to command"; aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1.2 and aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1.3 do not
aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps aristotle-politics-3.logic.r1, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if the virtues of a good man and a good magistrate are the same, then the virtue of a good man consists only in being able to command"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
